1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,380
Section 4

2
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:08,181
In today's class I'm going to talk about marine archaeology, the branch of archaeology

3
00:00:08,181 --> 00:00:14,320
focusing on human interaction with the sea, lakes and rivers.

4
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:19,124
It's the study of ships, cargoes, shipping facilities,

5
00:00:19,124 --> 00:00:22,260
and other physical remains.

6
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:26,994
I'll give you an example, then go on to show how this type of

7
00:00:26,994 --> 00:00:32,650
research is being transformed by the use of the latest technology.

8
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:37,507
Atlit-Yam was a village on the coast of the eastern Mediterranean,

9
00:00:37,507 --> 00:00:44,190
which seems to have been thriving until around 7,000 BC.

10
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:49,580
The residents kept cattle, caught fish and stored grain.

11
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:57,109
They had wells for fresh water, many of their houses were built around a courtyard

12
00:00:57,109 --> 00:00:59,800
and were constructed of stone.

13
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,350
The village contained an impressive monument:

14
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:09,267
seven half-tonne stones standing in a semicircle around a spring,

15
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:14,000
that might have been used for ceremonial purposes.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,843
Atlit-Yam may have been destroyed swiftly by a tsunami,

17
00:01:18,843 --> 00:01:26,377
or climate change may have caused glaciers to melt and sea levels to rise,

18
00:01:26,377 --> 00:01:28,530
flooding the village gradually.

19
00:01:28,530 --> 00:01:34,739
Whatever the cause, it now lies ten metres below the surface of the Mediterranean,

20
00:01:34,739 --> 00:01:38,730
buried under sand at the bottom of the sea.

21
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:46,666
It's been described as the largest and best preserved prehistoric settlement ever found on

22
00:01:46,666 --> 00:01:47,800
the seabed.

23
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:53,150
For marine archaeologists, Atlit-Yam is a treasure trove.

24
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:59,880
Research on the buildings, tools and the human remains has revealed how the bustling

25
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:01,323
village once functioned,

26
00:02:01,323 --> 00:02:06,130
and even what diseases some of its residents suffered from.

27
00:02:06,130 --> 00:02:13,569
But of course this is only one small village, one window into a lost

28
00:02:13,569 --> 00:02:14,100
world.

29
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:19,084
For a fuller picture, researchers need more sunken settlements,

30
00:02:19,084 --> 00:02:22,960
but the hard part is finding them.

31
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:27,929
Underwater research used to require divers to find shipwrecks or artefacts,

32
00:02:27,929 --> 00:02:34,254
but in the second half of the twentieth century, various types of underwater vehicles

33
00:02:34,254 --> 00:02:38,772
were developed, some controlled from a ship on the surface,

34
00:02:38,772 --> 00:02:41,031
and some of them autonomous,

35
00:02:41,031 --> 00:02:46,000
which means they don't need to be operated by a person.

36
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,768
Autonomous underwater vehicles,

37
00:02:47,768 --> 00:02:58,373
or AUVs, are used in the oil industry, for instance, to create maps of the seabed before rigs

38
00:02:58,373 --> 00:03:00,730
and pipelines are installed.

39
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:05,803
To navigate they use sensors such as compasses and sonar.

40
00:03:05,803 --> 00:03:09,354
Until relatively recently they were very expensive,

41
00:03:09,354 --> 00:03:16,455
and so heavy that they had to be launched from a large vessel with

42
00:03:16,455 --> 00:03:17,470
a winch.

43
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:25,310
But the latest AUVs are much easier to manoeuvre - they can be launched from the shore

44
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:27,270
or a small ship.

45
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:29,230
And they're much cheaper,

46
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:33,150
which makes them more accessible to research teams.

47
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:35,293
They're also very sophisticated.

48
00:03:35,293 --> 00:03:42,792
They can communicate with each other and, for example, workout the most efficient way

49
00:03:42,792 --> 00:03:44,935
to survey a site,

50
00:03:44,935 --> 00:03:49,220
or to find particular objects on the seabed.

51
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:52,844
Field tests show the approach can work.

52
00:03:52,844 --> 00:04:00,092
For example, in a trial in 2015, three AUVs searched for wrecks at Marzamemi,

53
00:04:00,092 --> 00:04:02,680
off the coast of Sicily.

54
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:08,432
The site is the final resting place of an ancient Roman ship,

55
00:04:08,432 --> 00:04:15,143
which sank in the sixth century AD while ferrying prefabricated marble elements for the

56
00:04:15,143 --> 00:04:17,540
construction of an early church.

57
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:24,942
The AUVs mapped the area in detail, finding other ships carrying columns of the

58
00:04:24,942 --> 00:04:26,000
same material.

59
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,641
Creating an internet in the sea for AUVs to communicate is no easy matter.

60
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:37,462
Wifi networks on land use electromagnetic waves,

61
00:04:37,462 --> 00:04:42,920
but in water these will only travel a few centimetres.

62
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:48,416
Instead, a more complex mix of technologies is required.

63
00:04:48,416 --> 00:04:56,966
For short distances, AUVs can share data using light while acoustic waves are used

64
00:04:56,966 --> 00:05:00,020
to communicate over long distances.

65
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:07,378
But more creative solutions are also being developed, where an AUV working on the

66
00:05:07,378 --> 00:05:11,057
seabed offloads data to a second AUV,

67
00:05:11,057 --> 00:05:18,414
which then surfaces and beams the data home to the research team using a

68
00:05:18,414 --> 00:05:18,940
satellite.

69
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:26,350
There's also a system that enables AUVs to share information from seabed scans,

70
00:05:26,350 --> 00:05:28,060
and other data.

71
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:35,400
So if an AUV surveying the seabed finds an intriguing object, it can share

72
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:41,692
the coordinates of the object - that is, its position - with a nearby

73
00:05:41,692 --> 00:05:44,314
AUV that carries superior cameras,

74
00:05:44,314 --> 00:05:51,130
and arrange for that AUV to make a closer inspection of the object.

75
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:58,950
Marine archaeologists are excited about the huge potential of these AUVs for their discipline.

76
00:05:58,950 --> 00:06:05,316
One site where they're going to be deployed is the Gulf of Baratti, off

77
00:06:05,316 --> 00:06:06,680
the Italian coast.

78
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:15,248
In 1974, a 2,000-year-old Roman vessel was discovered here, in 18 metres of

79
00:06:15,248 --> 00:06:15,860
water.

80
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:24,040
When it sank, it was carrying medical goods, in wooden or tin receptacles.

81
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:30,835
Its cargo gives us insight into the treatments available all those years ago, including

82
00:06:30,835 --> 00:06:37,629
tablets that are thought to have been dissolved to form a cleansing liquid for

83
00:06:37,629 --> 00:06:38,600
the eyes.

84
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:44,700
Other Roman ships went down nearby, taking their cargoes with them.

85
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:48,560
Some held huge pots made of terracotta.

86
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:53,524
Some were used for transporting cargoes of olive oil,

87
00:06:53,524 --> 00:06:55,730
and others held wine.

88
00:06:55,730 --> 00:07:00,269
In many cases it's only these containers that remain,

89
00:07:00,269 --> 00:07:06,320
while the wooden ships have been buried under silt on the seabed.

90
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:09,310
Another project that's about to...
